**Goal** — Add the injectable, bounded, header-safe HTTP client for koda's response envelope.

**Motivation** — Today a note saved in nax chat or a `nax run` lives only in one machine's mirror (`local`) or in an MCP server that koda cannot read.

**Approach** —
- URL: `url` with trailing `/` removed, then `/`, then `path`; query keys appended in insertion order with `URLSearchParams`, keys whose value is `undefined` skipped.
- Headers: the configured headers plus `accept: application/json`; a `post` adds `content-type: application/json` and the JSON-encoded body.
- A 2xx answer whose body parses as JSON resolves `{ status, data: body.data }`.
- A 2xx answer whose body is not JSON throws `NaxError` `MEMORY_KODA_BAD_RESPONSE`.
- A non-2xx answer throws `NaxError` `MEMORY_KODA_HTTP` with `context.status` set to the HTTP status and the message `[memory] koda <METHOD> <path> answered <status>: <message>`, where `<message>` is the body's `message` when it is a string, else the response's status text.
- No answer within `timeoutMs` aborts the request and throws `MEMORY_KODA_TIMEOUT`; a fetch that rejects throws `MEMORY_KODA_UNREACHABLE` with the rejection as `cause`.
- `kodaStatusOf(error)` returns `context.status` of a `MEMORY_KODA_HTTP` error, else `undefined`.
- Header values never appear in any error message, error context or log line.

The host builds the client with `MEMORY_KODA_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS` (4 s), well under sync's 10 s per-operation race (`MEMORY_OP_TIMEOUT_MS`), so a save's two requests (project id, then `POST memory`) settle inside one race.

Every path is relative to `memory.koda.url`, the API root including koda's global prefix (`https://koda.example/api`); a success body is `{ "ret": 0, "data": <payload> }`; an error body is `{ "ret": <code>, "message": "<text>" }`. Use a cancellable deadline that rejects even when the injected fetch ignores its AbortSignal; consume losing promises and clean up timers. Do not reinterpret application ret as an HTTP status.

**Scope** — In: koda-http.ts and its unit tests. Out: provider write rejection classification (US-005/US-006), config trust policy (US-001/US-012), provider composition (US-009). Retries or backoff inside the provider: a transient failure leaves the entry in the outbox for the next sync.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export interface KodaResponse { readonly status: number; readonly data: unknown }
export interface KodaClient {
  get(path: string, query?: Readonly<Record<string, string | number | undefined>>): Promise<KodaResponse>;
  post(path: string, body: unknown): Promise<KodaResponse>;
}
export function createKodaClient(input: {
  readonly url: string;
  readonly headers: Readonly<Record<string, string>>;
  readonly timeoutMs: number;
}): KodaClient;
export function kodaStatusOf(error: unknown): number | undefined;
export const MEMORY_KODA_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS = 4000;
export const _kodaClientDeps: { fetch: (input: string, init: RequestInit) => Promise<Response> };
```

**Goal** — Add pure feed parsing, per-slug NOTE reconciliation and incremental DECISION merging.

**Motivation** — The provider seam, the outbox and the sync loop were built for exactly this provider; `local` never exercises a remote cursor and `mcp` has none, so this is also the first provider that pulls.

**Approach** — nax-agent's sync applies a page's notes by slug, and a tombstone deletes the slug's note unconditionally (`syncMemory`, `packages/nax-agent/src/memory/sync.ts:332`). koda's feed carries rows for ids the mirror no longer holds (a superseded predecessor; an older retired note soft-deleted later), so applying rows one by one would let such a row delete a newer note. The mapping therefore reconciles a page into at most one note per slug, with these rules, in this order:

| # | Row | Effect |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | `kind` is not `"NOTE"`, or `scope` is not the page's scope | not a note of this page (DECISION rows go to the decision merge) |
| R2 | `subject` fails `MEMORY_SLUG_RE`, `predicate` is not one of `user`, `feedback`, `project`, `reference`, or a required field (`id`, `subject`, `predicate`, `object`, `description`, `updatedAt`, `status`) is missing or not a string | skipped and counted |
| R3 | `status` is `"superseded"` | adds no note. When its `id` equals the slug's current id (the current note of this page if one is set, else the mirror's note id), the slug's current id becomes *unknown*: its successor may be an active row or a tombstone later in the page |
| R4 | `status` is `"active"` and `deletedAt` is absent | becomes the slug's current note, status `active` |
| R5 | `status` is `"rejected"`, or `deletedAt` is present | a tombstone, applied only when its `id` equals the slug's current id (the current note of this page if one is set, else the mirror's note id for the slug), or when R3 made that id unknown. A tombstone that applies sets the current note to the row with status `rejected` (`deleted` when `deletedAt` is present); one that does not apply is ignored |
| R6 | output | the current note of every slug that has one, in slug code-point order |

A row maps to a `MemoryNote` as: `id` = row `id`; `slug` = `subject`; `type` = `predicate`; `scope` = the page's scope; `description` = `description`; `body` = `object`; `updatedAt` = `updatedAt`; `source` = `sourceType` with whitespace runs collapsed to one space, cut to `MEMORY_SOURCE_MAX_CHARS`, or `"koda"` when absent or blank.

The mirror ids R5 compares against are read before the page is reconciled: the provider reads the mirror note of every distinct slug the page's rows of this scope name, through the `readNote` it was given, and passes the ids as a map.

A local edit that koda answered `202` sits in the mirror as `pending` until the proposal resolves; a pull whose page carries the slug's unchanged active row (R4) replaces the pending edit with that row. The approved row arrives later through the feed. This is accepted behaviour.

**Decisions.** `DECISION` rows are merged only on project pages. A `DECISION` row whose `id`, `subject`, `predicate`, `object`, `updatedAt` or `status` is missing or not a string is skipped and counted with the note skips. A page with no valid `DECISION` row answers `decisions` undefined, so `decisions.jsonl` is not rewritten. A page with one or more merges them into the mirror's current decisions (`readDecisions`; sync writes `decisions.jsonl` after each page, so a later page sees the earlier one) by `id`: an `active` row with no `deletedAt` sets `{ id, subject, predicate, object, updatedAt, status: "active" }`; any other row removes its id. The merged list is ordered by `updatedAt` descending then `id`, and cut to `MEMORY_KODA_DECISIONS_MAX` (500). Rows of other kinds (`FACT`, `PREFERENCE`, `CONSTRAINT`, `INCIDENT_PATTERN`) are ignored.

`MEMORY_NOTE_TYPES` is not exported from the `@nathapp/nax-agent` entry, which is why `KODA_NOTE_TYPES` is declared here (the pattern `NOTE_TYPES` follows in `src/cli/memory-cli-deps.ts`).

**Scope** — In: pure mapping module and unit tests; no external calls. Out: kodaHitOf/search (US-006), feed I/O and mirror reads (US-007), proposal resolution (US-008). Mirroring koda memory kinds other than `NOTE` and `DECISION` (`FACT`, `PREFERENCE`, `CONSTRAINT`, `INCIDENT_PATTERN`). Keeping a pending local edit visible while its proposal is open: a pull carrying the slug's unchanged active row replaces it until the approved row arrives. A supersession and a retirement of one note that land on two different export pages: R3/R5 track an unknown current id within one page only, so the stale active note survives until the next change to that slug.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export interface KodaMemoryItem {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly kind: string;               // "NOTE" | "DECISION" | other koda kinds
  readonly scope: string;              // "project" | "user"
  readonly subject: string;
  readonly predicate: string;
  readonly object: string;
  readonly description?: string;
  readonly status: string;             // "active" | "superseded" | "rejected"
  readonly updatedAt: string;
  readonly deletedAt?: string | null;
  readonly sourceType?: string;
}
export const KODA_NOTE_TYPES = ["user", "feedback", "project", "reference"] as const satisfies readonly MemoryNoteType[];
export const MEMORY_KODA_DECISIONS_MAX = 500;
/** Shape check only: an array of objects. Throws MEMORY_KODA_BAD_RESPONSE when `value` is not an array. */
export function parseKodaItems(value: unknown): readonly KodaMemoryItem[];
export function reconcileKodaNotes(
  items: readonly KodaMemoryItem[],
  scope: MemoryScope,
  mirrorIds: ReadonlyMap<string, string>,   // slug -> the mirror note's id
): { readonly notes: readonly MemoryNote[]; readonly skipped: number };
export function mergeKodaDecisions(
  current: readonly MemoryDecision[],
  items: readonly KodaMemoryItem[],
): { readonly decisions: readonly MemoryDecision[] | undefined; readonly skipped: number };
```

**Goal** — Add durable, versioned records for 202 saves so rejected proposals can later be reconciled.

**Motivation** — Today a note saved in nax chat or a `nax run` lives only in one machine's mirror (`local`) or in an MCP server that koda cannot read.

**Approach** — A `202` save means koda opened an approval request and wrote no row. If it is executed, the row appears in the export feed and a later pull replaces the mirror's pending note. If it is rejected, nothing ever appears, so the provider records each `202` save in `koda-approvals.json` in the project mirror directory:

```json
{ "version": 1, "approvals": [ { "approvalId": "apr_1", "scope": "project", "slug": "replay-budget", "clientId": "c_9" } ] }
```

A record for a scope and slug that already has one replaces it. The file is written to a temporary path and renamed into place (the `mcp-id-map.ts` pattern). An unreadable or invalid file reads as no approvals and is logged at warn once per `KodaApprovals` instance.

Keep list order meaningful for the later oldest-record-first resolution budget. Use the project mirror directory for records of both scopes, not separate user-scope files. Reuse the established atomic persistence pattern and logger contract.

**Scope** — In: koda-approvals.ts, its persistence seam and tests. Out: recording saves in the provider (US-005), checking remote approval status/restoration (US-008), reset preservation and relink deletion (US-010/US-011). De-duplicating koda approval requests when a `202` save is re-pushed because its first answer was lost.

**Interface** —
```typescript
export interface KodaApproval { readonly approvalId: string; readonly scope: MemoryScope; readonly slug: string; readonly clientId: string }
export interface KodaApprovals {
  list(): Promise<readonly KodaApproval[]>;
  record(entry: KodaApproval): Promise<void>;
  drop(approvalIds: readonly string[]): Promise<void>;
}
export function createKodaApprovals(input: { readonly dir: string; readonly logger: MemoryLogger }): KodaApprovals;
export const _kodaApprovalsDeps: { write(path: string, text: string): Promise<void>; rename(from: string, to: string): Promise<void> };
```

**Goal** — Prevent non-global layers from choosing the koda credential destination or headers and expose all koda settings in config descriptions.

**Motivation** — The user ruled (2026-11-02) that nax chat and koda threads share ONE memory with koda as the source of truth, and that nax pulls a per-project mirror (pull architecture, design 6.5).

**Approach** — **Global only.** `rejectGlobalOnlyKeys(layerConf, layerName)` (`src/config/global-only-keys.ts`) also throws `NaxError` `MEMORY_KODA_CONFIG_NOT_GLOBAL` with the message `memory.koda.<key> belongs in ~/.nax/config.json and cannot be set in <layerName>` when the layer sets `memory.koda.url` or `memory.koda.headers`. It already runs on the project, profile, package and CLI layers (`loader.ts:139`, `:197`, `:346`, `:461`, `profile.ts:112`). `memory.koda.project` is allowed in every layer. `nax config` already masks every value of a `headers` map (`maskProfileValues`, `src/cli/config-profile.ts`), so no masking change is needed.

`FIELD_DESCRIPTIONS` (`src/cli/config-descriptions.ts`) gains `memory.koda`, `memory.koda.url`, `memory.koda.project` and `memory.koda.headers`, and the `memory.provider` description names `"koda"`.

Global/project example:
```jsonc
// ~/.nax/config.json (global)
"memory": {
  "koda": {
    "url": "https://koda.example/api",
    "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer <agent key>" }
  }
}
// <repo>/.nax/config.json (project) — what `nax memory link rs-stock` writes
"memory": { "provider": "koda", "koda": { "project": "rs-stock" } }
```

Extend the existing global-only guard instead of duplicating layer checks. It must preserve trust/auth refusals and return allowed inputs unchanged. Validate the nested presence of url/headers safely against raw layer data; do not log their values. Tests extend existing global-only/config-description suites and exercise loadConfig with a temporary global directory so literal credentials do not reach the real user's configuration.

**Scope** — In: global-only-keys.ts, FIELD_DESCRIPTIONS and guard/loader integration tests. Out: schema and runtime types (US-001), HTTP headers/errors (US-002), writing raw project config during link (US-010), changes to the existing masking implementation. Credential sources for `memory.koda.headers` other than literal values: no `${VAR}` expansion and no `auth`/exec credential source.

**Interface** — Preserve the existing guard signature:
```typescript
export function rejectGlobalOnlyKeys<T extends Record<string, unknown>>(layerConf: T, layerName: string): T;
```
New refusal code: `MEMORY_KODA_CONFIG_NOT_GLOBAL`. New description keys: `memory.koda`, `memory.koda.url`, `memory.koda.project`, `memory.koda.headers`.
